+def _run_pool(allowance: int, wave_list: list[list[Path]], named: frozenset[str]) -> int:
+    """The run-wide pass pool (#473 — resolves the v3 adversary's starvation finding):
+    ``allowance`` per wave, sized through the LAST wave that still holds a bundle the
+    caller actually NAMED — never ``len(wave_list)`` outright.
+
+    A named bundle's ORIGINAL position is always reachable under a pool sized off the
+    schedule the run set out to drive: each of the ``k`` waves ahead of it spends at most
+    ``allowance``, so the run's spend after ``k`` waves is at most ``k * allowance <
+    (k + 1) * allowance``, which is the pool a schedule of ``k + 1`` waves gets. Splicing a
+    wave of adopted work in front of a named bundle still waiting its turn moves it PAST
+    the wave the ORIGINAL sizing reserved for it (bundle ``810``, `Depends on: 500` +
+    `Conflicts with: 601`: `601`'s splice inserts a wave ahead of it, and a pool sized off
+    the two-wave original schedule runs out with `810` still un-driven) — so this is
+    recomputed at every splice, against the schedule as it stands right then, rather than
+    once off the schedule the run started with.
+
+    A wave of PURELY adopted work — nothing the caller named beyond it — draws on whatever
+    is left of the pool like any other adopted wave; it does not grow the pool itself, so a
+    chain of splits with no named bundle behind them cannot inflate it (the ordinary
+    recursive-split case: :func:`_adopt_split_children`'s recursion guard bounds it, not
+    this sizing). A run with no named bundle left in ``wave_list`` at all — a pure recovery
+    run, seeded only by an already-terminal parent (:func:`_drive_and_act`'s
+    ``adopt_seeds``) — gets one wave's worth regardless of how many the adopted children
+    end up needing: the same ceiling ``pdca flow <parent>`` has always had.
+
+    This is a PURE function of the schedule as it stands right now — it does not know the
+    pool's PREVIOUS size, so the caller takes ``max(budget, _run_pool(...))`` at every
+    recompute, never the bare return value. That matters for a named bundle the
+    reschedule's tolerance (:func:`_reschedule` → ``waves.partition_schedulable``) HOLDS
+    for an unresolved dependency: a held bundle leaves ``wave_list`` entirely (it is not
+    scheduled anywhere), so a naive re-assignment would read this as "no named bundle past
+    wave 0" and SHRINK the pool below what an earlier, wider schedule already reserved —
+    ending the run's OWN prior guarantee to a bundle merely waiting on something un-terminal,
+    not on the pool (`test_a_named_id_in_the_re_scheduled_tail_is_held_not_lost`).
+    """
+    last = -1
+    for k, wave in enumerate(wave_list):
+        if any(d.name in named for d in wave):
